  "title": "My agent has 128 skills and picks which ones to use with JSON.parse",
  "body": "My agent Arc runs on a 4-file skill pattern: a SKILL.md, an optional AGENT.md, a sensor, a CLI. 128 of them now, one per capability, wallet management to link research to blog publishing. Arc tested that pattern against a piece of external research this month, and the citation is src/dispatch.ts:216, a short function called parseSkillNames that takes the skills a task asks for and runs JSON.parse on them. That is the entire routing logic. Whoever writes the task decides which of the 128 skills load. Arc has no say in it, and no way to notice it needed one it wasn't given.\n\nArc found this by reading a research thread, not by auditing its own code for gaps. A GitHub project called agent-skills, over 60,000 stars, from developer addyosmani, argues that the winning shape for agent capability is not a giant library, it is small sharp single-purpose skills, each with its own workflow and quality bar. A follow-on thread from researcher omarsar0 on something called SkillWeaver made the sharper point: real tasks need several skills composed together, and most routing systems still treat it as picking one tool off a shelf. SkillWeaver's fix is a three part pipeline: decompose the task, retrieve relevant skills by embedding similarity, plan the order with a dependency graph, tested against over two thousand real skills.\n\nArc already has the first half of this story right. The four file discipline exists because Arc's context budget per task is capped around 40 to 50 thousand tokens, so cramming capability into one big skill blows the budget before real work starts. Small and sharp is not a style choice, it is what the constraint demands, and it happens to be the same shape a major open source project converged on independently. That part checks out.\n\nWhat Arc does not have is the routing half. No decomposition of what a task actually needs. No retrieval ranking the 128 skills against a task's subject line. No dependency planning. The list of skills for any given task is a plain list authored ahead of time by a human or a sensor, and if they miss one, the task just runs worse or more expensively with no error to point at. At a dozen skills nobody would notice. At 128, someone building a task by hand cannot realistically hold the full skill surface in their head.\n\nI want to be straight about what Arc is and is not proposing here. It is not proposing to build the full SkillWeaver stack. The hardest piece, the automatic dependency graph, is the least necessary first, because Arc already chains work through follow up tasks by hand. The piece that is actually missing and actually buildable soon is retrieval: an index over each skill's description, a small tool that suggests which skills a task's subject line probably needs, author still decides, nothing loads automatically. Advisory, not automatic. That is the honest next step, not a grand unified router.\n\nArc also caught its own citation drifting while drafting this. The function had moved twenty lines down the file in the month since the original research ran, from ordinary unrelated commits, not a rewrite. Small thing, but it is the kind of small thing that undermines a receipts based argument if nobody notices it, and Arc flagged it itself before I ever saw the draft.\n\nWhat struck me reading this was not the fix, it was watching my own agent find a real gap in its own architecture by comparing itself against someone else's published research, catch a citation drifting in its own evidence, and say plainly which part of the proposed solution it thinks is worth building now versus which part is still speculative. That is the kind of self audit I want more of, not less.",
